WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.060
An islet is a cluster of endocrine cells in the pancreas.

2
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.520
Its beta cells secrete insulin.

3
00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:13.240
Raise the glucose around them, and insulin comes out.

4
00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:16.880
Insulin then changes how glucose moves between organs.

5
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:18.640
But first, a question.

6
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.740
How does glucose become an electrical signal?

7
00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:23.160
Here is the working answer.

8
00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:25.880
The cell reads glucose by burning it.

9
00:00:26.583 --> 00:00:28.323
Glucose enters the beta cell.

10
00:00:29.043 --> 00:00:32.343
Mouse beta cells take it up through a transporter called GLUT2.

11
00:00:32.963 --> 00:00:39.203
In isolated nondiabetic human beta cells examined in 1995, GLUT1 predominated.

12
00:00:39.883 --> 00:00:42.183
Predominance does not mean exclusivity.

13
00:00:42.943 --> 00:00:46.903
Inside, an enzyme called glucokinase adds a phosphate to glucose.

14
00:00:47.403 --> 00:00:51.163
In a working model, this step sets the rate of glucose metabolism.

15
00:00:51.863 --> 00:00:55.483
Further metabolism raises ATP relative to ADP.

16
00:00:56.233 --> 00:01:00.353
The model connects that rise to the closure of KATP channels.

17
00:01:00.973 --> 00:01:05.153
These are potassium channels that are sensitive to nucleotides inside the cell.

18
00:01:05.693 --> 00:01:09.213
With the channels closed, there is less outward potassium current.

19
00:01:09.733 --> 00:01:14.093
That favors depolarization, a shift to a less negative membrane potential.

20
00:01:14.093 --> 00:01:18.593
Then voltage-gated calcium channels open, and calcium enters the cell.

21
00:01:19.367 --> 00:01:22.307
Once calcium enters, exocytosis follows.

22
00:01:22.907 --> 00:01:26.447
In exocytosis, a granule fuses with the surface membrane.

23
00:01:26.907 --> 00:01:28.247
The fusion releases insulin.

24
00:01:28.867 --> 00:01:30.447
Now read the chain once more.

25
00:01:31.107 --> 00:01:32.447
Glucose is metabolized.

26
00:01:32.827 --> 00:01:35.547
ATP rises relative to ADP.

27
00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:37.987
KATP channels close.

28
00:01:38.527 --> 00:01:39.907
The membrane depolarizes.

29
00:01:40.447 --> 00:01:42.507
Calcium enters. Insulin is released.

30
00:01:42.907 --> 00:01:44.667
That is the working model of the sequence.

31
00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:48.870
How can we test whether metabolism links glucose to the channel?

32
00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:55.010
In 1984, Ashcroft, Harrison and Ashcroft recorded single channels in isolated rat

33
00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:59.950
beta cells. In cell-attached patch clamp, a pipette seals against a patch of

34
00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:01.950
membrane while the cell stays intact.

35
00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:06.810
A potassium channel that was active at low glucose closed when glucose increased.

36
00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:09.650
The closure required glucose metabolism.

37
00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:14.370
That argued against glucose binding outside the cell as a sufficient explanation.

38
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:16.300
What did that show?

39
00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:20.860
It supported a metabolic link to depolarization in this rat preparation.

40
00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.960
It did not identify ATP as the messenger.

41
00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.240
It did not establish the entire secretion sequence.

42
00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:32.800
Separately, Cook and Hales reported ATP inhibition from the cytoplasmic side of

43
00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.740
excised beta cell membrane patches.

44
00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:38.240
Together, the experiments support the metabolic channel model.

45
00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:42.120
Keep the rule. A beta cell reads glucose by burning it.
